  <Patch Id="HrjXoFxJg5yTRjEXOF12Kv">
    <Canvas Id="TdsUiluAib2aHgmd4ufvnF" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="Gdo32fBDRDPvP0JiAtCfPN">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="SBlWRAaMNxqalbiySBbFGH">
        <Canvas Id="PhiiqRuPELAZVpAudCqZr1" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="DHYilQNozrcj7pIFEZhST6" Bounds="-880,-140,680,980" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="TUTORIAL 14 - What if the world does not fit in memory&#xD;&#xA;the question decides what gets read&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Somewhere on a server sits a file of 18,588,472 bytes. This patch never downloads it. And yet:&#xD;&#xA;point at Europe on the left and a hand-painted piece of it appears on the right, fetched from the&#xD;&#xA;MIDDLE of that file - because your question decided which bytes were worth asking for.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;FIRST, CONSENT: opening this patch performs zero network requests. The grid you see is local - a&#xD;&#xA;small index file listing 25 pieces of Europe and where each one lives inside the big file. Flip&#xD;&#xA;FETCH when you are ready; from then on, each piece you point at costs ONE request.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THEN POINT. Your cursor is the question, as it has been since chapter 01. The patch asks the index&#xD;&#xA;'which piece covers this point?' with the same Contains you met in chapter 03, reads that piece's&#xD;&#xA;address out of its attributes (chapter 07's features, chapter 09's files), builds one request for&#xD;&#xA;EXACTLY THOSE BYTES, and draws what comes back.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE NUMBERS ARE THE LESSON. Watch the right side:&#xD;&#xA;  the file          18,588,472 bytes - and it never moves&#xD;&#xA;  this answer       asked-for and received must MATCH, around 7,000-15,000 bytes&#xD;&#xA;  read              about 0.05% of the file, per question&#xD;&#xA;  the server said   206 - which means 'here is the part you asked for', not 200, 'here is&#xD;&#xA;                    everything'. If you ever see 200 with eighteen million bytes received, the&#xD;&#xA;                    server ignored the address and sent the whole file - the exact thing this&#xD;&#xA;                    chapter exists to avoid.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHEN IT FAILS, IT SAYS SO. Point outside the grid: no piece covers that point, nothing is read,&#xD;&#xA;and the patch says so instead of pretending an empty success (the same lesson the first&#xD;&#xA;Explanation learned about missing files). If the network fails, the status line carries the&#xD;&#xA;server's answer, whatever it is. If what arrives is not a picture, the decoder will complain&#xD;&#xA;loudly rather than draw nothing quietly.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;HOW THIS RELATES TO WHAT YOU KNOW. The two Explanations proved the facts this chapter turns into&#xD;&#xA;action: 'You do not have to read everything' showed that a good LAYOUT lets a small question cost&#xD;&#xA;a small read; 'Sharper is a different question' showed that SHARPNESS picks how much world one&#xD;&#xA;fetch carries. Here both become one move: where + how sharp = which piece = which bytes.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;ONE HONEST SIMPLIFICATION. The index on the left was extracted for this lesson by a recorded&#xD;&#xA;script (lab\probe-pmtiles.py) and rewritten as GeoJSON so you can open it in a text editor. A&#xD;&#xA;real archive of this kind carries its own directory INSIDE the file - a client reads a 127-byte&#xD;&#xA;header, then a few KB of directory, then the piece; three ranged reads, no side file. The&#xD;&#xA;simplification changes who parses the directory, not how the file answers.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WORDS FOR WHAT YOU JUST DID - the vocabulary, now that you have the experience: asking for bytes&#xD;&#xA;N..M of a file is an HTTP RANGE REQUEST; the server's 206 is PARTIAL CONTENT; the big file is a&#xD;&#xA;PMTILES archive (a pyramid of map tiles in one file); and files ARRANGED so questions become byte&#xD;&#xA;addresses are called CLOUD-OPTIMIZED - COG for rasters, GeoParquet for tables, PMTiles for tile&#xD;&#xA;pyramids. None of them needs a special server. That is the entire idea: no tile service, no API -&#xD;&#xA;an ordinary file, an ordinary web server, and a question that knows its own address.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Explanation You do not have to read everything   the layout half of this idea, on local files&#xD;&#xA;  Explanation Sharper is a different question      the sharpness half, on a real map&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 09                                      what any file that reaches your patch owes you&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Data: Map tiles by Stamen Design (CC BY 3.0), archived by Cooper Hewitt / maplibre demotiles;&#xD;&#xA;fetched piecewise from their public archive. The index: Assets\watercolor-directory.geojson,&#xD;&#xA;derived by lab\probe-pmtiles.py, same credit. Zero requests until FETCH.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
